{
  // ===========================================================================
  // Solar System Explorer — configuration
  //
  // This file is read once when the app starts. It is JSON with comments:
  //   // line comments, /* block comments */ and trailing commas are all fine.
  //
  // Every setting is optional. Anything missing, misspelled or out of range is
  // ignored and the built-in default is used instead, so the app always starts.
  // The haze, cloud and opacity numbers are fractions from 0 (0%) to 1 (100%);
  // the spacecraft size is a whole-number level from 1 to 100.
  //
  // The same values are also live in the UI: the sliders in the planet panel
  // and the two side-panel opacity sliders, so you can preview a change and
  // then write it down here.
  // ===========================================================================

  // The display language. Supported: "en" (English) or "zh" (Hanyu / Chinese).
  "Language": "en",

  // --- Earth's atmosphere --------------------------------------------------
  // How heavily the blue air hides the surface. 0 = bare continents and oceans,
  // 1 = the full glowing limb.
  "Earth Atmosphere Haze": 0.45,

  // --- Earth's clouds ------------------------------------------------------
  // How much of the sky the clouds fill: 0 = a few scattered wisps,
  // 1 = a near-continuous sheet.
  "Earth Cloud Coverage": 0.3,
  // How solid the cloud deck is: 0 = clear, 1 = the full cloud cover.
  "Earth Cloud Opacity": 0.5,
  // How much the cloud shapes swirl and re-form: 0 = frozen mid-drift,
  // 1 = the liveliest.
  "Earth Cloud Dynamics": 0.5,

  // --- Earth's terrain -----------------------------------------------------
  // How much of the real elevation model (a global NASA / USGS height map) is
  // applied to the surface itself. 0 = a smooth globe, 1 = the full vertical
  // exaggeration (160x), which is what makes mountain ranges, plateaus and
  // canyons stand out of the sphere. The relief is exaggerated because the true
  // thing is only 0.14% of Earth's radius — a fraction of a pixel from orbit.
  "Earth Terrain Relief": 0.25,

  // --- Earth's seafloor ----------------------------------------------------
  // How far the real seafloor is displaced below sea level, from 0 (a flat
  // floor) to 1 (the full vertical exaggeration). This is independent of the
  // land's "Earth Terrain Relief", so the drained ocean can be dialled in on its
  // own. The depth comes from the public-domain ETOPO 2022 bathymetry grid.
  "Earth Seafloor Relief": 0.4,
  // How opaque the ocean is: 1 keeps the shipped ocean exactly as it looks, and
  // 0 drains it to reveal the seafloor. It is a nominal visibility control, not
  // a physical model of underwater light. The planet panel's slider is the live
  // version of this value.
  "Earth Seawater Opacity": 1,
  // How full the ocean is in the "volumetric" mode: 1 = the water surface sits
  // at mean sea level (the full depth), 0 = it has dropped to the deepest floor
  // and the seafloor is fully exposed. It is independent of "Earth Seawater
  // Opacity": the level moves the surface, the opacity thins the water.
  "Earth Sea Level": 1,
  // How strong the waves are on the "volumetric" ocean, 0 to 1. One slider
  // scales the wave height, the flow speed, the churning chop and the coastal
  // surf together. 0 = completely still; 0.25 (the default) already reads as a
  // living sea; 1 = the tuned maximum, a rough sea that breaks on the coast.
  // It has no effect in the "surface" mode, which has no waves.
  "Earth Wave Intensity": 0.25,
  // How fine and dense the spectral sea's rings and arcs are, 0 to 1. 0 (the
  // default) keeps the normal shipped ring size; raising it scrunches the crests
  // progressively smaller and more numerous, up to a very fine, dense sea. It has
  // no effect in the "surface" mode or on the "ripples" wave model. The planet
  // panel's "Ring detail" slider is the live version of this value.
  "Earth Swell Detail": 0,
  // How strongly the "volumetric" ocean reflects the sky, 0 to 1. This is the
  // Fresnel sheen that makes the sea read as water rather than plastic from a
  // high altitude: 1 is the full physical weight, 0 leaves only the dark body
  // colour and the Sun's glint, and 0.6 (the default) sits just under the
  // atmosphere's own glow. It applies to both wave models and has no effect in
  // the "surface" mode. The planet panel's "Sky reflection" slider is the live
  // version of this value.
  "Earth Sky Reflection": 0.6,
  // The body colour of the "volumetric" ocean. "natural" (the default, and the
  // first option) leaves the day map's own ocean colour exactly as it is, with no
  // recolouring; the others recolour it, and are two-tone — a shallow shelf colour
  // falling to a deep basin colour. "blue" is a teal shelf over a deep navy
  // basin; "deep" is a deeper, more saturated pair; "abyss" is a steel shelf over
  // an almost black ink-blue basin; and "tropical" is a bright lagoon turquoise
  // over a slightly deeper teal. They change only the water's colour, never its
  // waves or reflections. It has no effect in the "surface" mode. The planet
  // panel's "Ocean colour" switcher is the live version of this value, and it is
  // shown only while the "volumetric" mode is active.
  "Ocean Colour": "natural",
  // How wide the "volumetric" ocean's shallow-shelf band is, from 0 to 0.2.
  // This is the water depth (as a fraction of the ocean's full depth) at which
  // the shallow shelf colour has fully given way to the deep basin colour, so a
  // small value leaves only a thin coastal shelf — the deep ocean stays one
  // uniform colour, as it looks from orbit — while a large value lets the
  // shallow colour cover much of the ocean and shows the seafloor's shape.
  // 0.007 (the default) is a narrow shelf; 0.05 is very wide. It applies to
  // every "Ocean Colour" except "natural", which keeps the day map's own colour.
  "Earth Shallow Depth": 0.007,
  // Which wave model the "volumetric" ocean draws. "spectral" (the default) is
  // the living, procedural sea — a directional wave spectrum that keeps changing
  // shape, never a moving picture. "ripples" is the original tiling ripple normal
  // map slid across the surface: cheaper, but the pattern repeats. The planet
  // panel's switcher is the live version of this value, and it is shown only
  // while the "volumetric" mode is active.
  "Wave Style": "spectral",
  // Which ocean implementation draws the sea. "surface" (the default) fades the
  // single painted ocean surface in place to reveal the seafloor; "volumetric"
  // draws a real transparent water layer over the drained seafloor, which looks
  // richer but costs an extra draw call. The planet panel's switcher is the live
  // version of this value.
  "Seawater Mode": "surface",

  // --- Side panels ---------------------------------------------------------
  // Opacity of the left "Controls" panel and the right information panel.
  // 0 = invisible (its header controls stay usable), 1 = fully opaque.
  "SimulationControls Opacity": 0.75,
  "PlanetPanel Opacity": 0.75,

  // --- Spacecraft ----------------------------------------------------------
  // The ship's size, as one of a hundred levels: 1 is the smallest (1% of the
  // model) and 100 is the model's full size, with each level at n/100 of that
  // size. 10 (10% of full size) is the default. The model itself never changes.
  "Spacecraft Size": 10
}
